A||||15||webbee||Cameras for pics||||5||
Z||000000||webbee||07-04-2000||08:25 PM||||Are you all using digital cameras for the posted pics? If yes, what kind of camera are you useing?. Do you like it, would you buy it again?<P>If you are scanning them what kind of scanner are you using? Like it/buy it again?<P>I'd like to play too, but no equipment.<p>[This message has been edited by webbee (edited 07-04-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gokart||07-04-2000||08:33 PM||||I'm posting with a digital camera we just received. It's a DSCPRO and so far were very happy with it and the qualitys not bad as you can see from pics I posted. I believe it's inexpensive too at $89.00.||||reg||14
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||07-04-2000||08:37 PM||||Put out a APB on a guy name Terry!!!!<BR>he's got one nice digital camera, but I think he's a pro   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> the price of his is way out of my pocket!!!!.I think most of the guys have digital cams. But this is a good topic so lets see what all the members use.<P>Most of my pics I have put on a disk<P>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 07-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-04-2000||08:53 PM||||Scanners are dirt cheap right now, but I love my digital camera, a Olympus D-400. I wouldn't buy anything under 1 megapixel resolution (mine is 1.3). It's a good idea to buy an extra memory flash card too. There are some limitations with a digital camera, but with a little practice, I've been able to get some really good pics.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||07-04-2000||08:59 PM||||uh oh - DXR_DAD caught me........he's seen mine -- I am only a genuine imitation of a reasonable facsimile of a semi-professional (I have a web design business....) -- I use the Sony FD-91 - the retail list is $999.00 but I got mine off of eBay for $750.00. -- it captures on a floppy disc in a variety of resolutions as well as being able to make mpeg movies complete with sound........I reviewed many cameras and selected this one because of it's versatility -- and I'll certainly stick with it!! Thanks Bill, for the compliment!!<P>------------------<BR>My '87 Fiero GT -  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero/</A>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/spooky3.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||07-04-2000||09:41 PM||||Kodak 280 zoom 2.0 megapixel resolution. The higher the resolution the better.<P>HP 6200c scanner<P>Advantix 3700 APS camera processed to Picture CD.<P>Advantix film drive scanner.<P>Adobe photo software<P>The digital camera produced a better quality photo when using maximum resolution than photos put on the Picture CD.<P>At High resolution a 32MB picture card only holds 51 photos.  I think the 32 MB card was ~$100.   The cards are not cheap, but the camera only came with an 8MB card.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||07-04-2000||09:53 PM||||I use a Sony Mavica MCV-FD7 an older version of tgowens camera. It doesn't do the Mpeg movies. I,ve had it about two years and it has held up well to quiet a bit of abuse. When I bought it the price was $699.00. today the sell for around $550.00. <BR>Here is a review:  <A HREF="http://pcphotoreview.com/dc/reviews/pro/mvc_fd7.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://pcphotoreview.com/dc/reviews/pro/mvc_fd7.htm</A> <BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||07-04-2000||10:00 PM||||I use a Toshiba PDR M-1. It's a 1.3 (I think) megapixel. Cost $299. I think it's out of production, though. Olympus also sells a 1.3. Seems like it was about the same price. <BR>Cool thing about mine was that it was only supposed to come with a 4 MB card (which was still in its sleeve, in the box. <BR>Except I bought the last one in the store, which happened to be the display model, which happened to have a 16 MB card already installed. <BR>I felt a bit like a crook, until I reminded myself that they weren't willing to discount the price at all, since I was buying the demo.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||07-04-2000||10:19 PM||||Kodak DC210+. It uses the compact flash memory. I like it because in standard resolution a 16 meg card holds about 200 pictures and about 50 high res. pictures. My in-laws have a Sony FD91 that I also use- both are very high quality and easy to used, it just depends on if you need the convenience of the floppy disk or capacity of the flash cards.||||reg||1
Z||000009||BN Boomer||07-05-2000||03:02 AM||||I use a Sony Mavica FD-88. 1.3 megapixels. Paid $699 for it at Circuit City a few months ago. I've been really happy with it so far. I also like the fact that it use inexpensive and easily procured floppy disks instead of the memory cards.||||reg||1
Z||000010||LAMBO||07-05-2000||01:17 PM||||I just got a Sony DCS 120 digital camcorder/camera. It takes stills at 640x480 as wall as digital footage on miniDV cassettes. I needed both but didn't want to buy two seperate pieces of equipment. So far I love it. The memory sticks are very convenient with a floppy adapter. The camcorder/camera is super small which is also very convenient.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fotofrank||07-05-2000||01:40 PM|||| I use an Olimpus C2000 as my digital camera (2.1Mpixels). I also scan slides on a Polaroid film/slide scanner. My flatbed scanner is an old color HP at 400dpi. I use Pentax 35mm cameras and lenses. I am a true beliver in PhotoShop 5 and "touch-up" all my digital photos. Proper color levels,color balance and density(contrast/brightness) are a must. It makes the image "pop". Remember for the web convert to "index color" at 72dpi (monitors never display more than 72 dpi)Also use the unsharp filter to sharpen your photo.Size the resulting photograph at about 5X7 using the image size tool under image.Use Image Size to get a 72 dpi image also. This will alow a good looking image and not cause a "scrol" to see it all.<BR>Most all of what I mentioned is found in every photo editing software package. If you have any questions feel free to ask. To see my work visit me home page. <A HREF="http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fotofrank.homestead.com/foto1.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fotofrank (edited 07-05-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000012||FieroSTETZ||07-05-2000||03:48 PM||||I have a 3dfx voodoo3 3500 tv, I use my video camera, take footage i like, and capture stills from the video footage- allows me to get some awesome shots you cant usually get with a normal still cam.||||reg||1
Z||000013||TheFiero64||07-05-2000||07:59 PM||||I use a <B>Polaroid PDC640</B>. I am very happy with it. It was a Christmas gift <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Joshua Bumpus<BR>New Bedford, MA<BR><B>88 V6 + many more</B>||||reg||10
Z||000014||Cliff Pennock||07-05-2000||08:37 PM||||Hmmmm, I just take pictures with my trusty ol' <B>Canon T70</B> and then scan the pictures with a high-res flatbed scanner. I know - not really what you would expect from a computer-guy but the results are unmatched by any digital cam I've seen so far...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Robert||07-05-2000||08:49 PM||||My toy is the Olympus C-2020-z.   2.1 magapixel.  I paid $680 a few months ago on Ebay when the list price was $800, you can get one now for about $560 with a list price of $700.||||reg||1
